<div class="takeaways"><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul>
  <li>Sports psychology career paths split into two jobs: licensed psychologist, who holds a doctorate and can diagnose and treat, and mental performance consultant, who holds a master's plus CMPC and trains mental skills.</li>
  <li>A bachelor's alone qualifies you for five support roles: performance coach assistant, rehabilitation assistant, research assistant, youth sports counselor, and fitness program coordinator. It does not qualify you for independent practice.</li>
  <li>The master's plus CMPC route reaches paid consulting work in roughly four to six years. The doctoral route to licensure takes eight to twelve.</li>
  <li>Pay varies by setting more than by title. Pro teams and private practice reach $250,000 at the top, while entry roles start around $45,000 to $70,000.</li>
  <li>Jobs are found where the work happens: the AASP Career Center, USAJOBS, NCAA Market, and Teamwork Online. Search "mental performance consultant" rather than "sports psychologist", because most postings use the consultant title.</li>
